BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//Littoralicious//Terroir Paris//EN
X-WR-CALNAME:Paris — eat & drink checklist
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-1@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Baguette de tradition — The legally defined loaf: flour\, water\, salt\, yeast\, nothing else\, never frozen.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ba-GET de tra-dee-SYON. The one food Paris has written into law\; break the heel off on the way home. Best: Du Pain et des Idées\, 10th — in the larder\; or any boulangerie that bakes on the premises
LOCATION:Du Pain et des Idées\, 10th — in the larder\; or any boulangerie that bakes on the premises
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Du+Pain+et+des+Idees+34+rue+Yves+Toudic+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-2@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Jambon-beurre — A split baguette\, butter\, boiled ham. Nothing else.
DESCRIPTION:Say: zhom-BON burr. The porters' sandwich of Les Halles\, still the benchmark the country measures itself by. Best: Le Petit Vendôme\, rue des Capucines — at the counter
LOCATION:Le Petit Vendôme\, rue des Capucines — at the counter
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Petit+Vendome+8+rue+des+Capucines+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-3@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Steak au poivre — A thick steak crusted in cracked pepper\, finished with a cognac-cream sauce\, with frites.
DESCRIPTION:Say: stek oh PWAHV. The bistro plate Paris argues over most\; the one on the rue Paul Bert is the usual answer. Best: Bistrot Paul Bert\, 11th — book a few days ahead
LOCATION:Bistrot Paul Bert\, 11th — book a few days ahead
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bistrot+Paul+Bert+18+rue+Paul+Bert+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-4@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Steak-frites — Entrecôte or onglet seared hard\, a raft of thin fries\, the green-butter or pepper sauce.
DESCRIPTION:Say: stek FREET. Judged always by the fries\; one room serves nothing else. Best: Le Relais de l'Entrecôte\, rue Saint-Benoît — one menu\, no bookings
LOCATION:Le Relais de l'Entrecôte\, rue Saint-Benoît — one menu\, no bookings
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Relais+de+l%27Entrecote+20+rue+Saint-Benoit+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-5@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Steak tartare — Raw beef cut by hand\, seasoned with capers\, shallot and mustard\, with frites.
DESCRIPTION:Say: stek tar-TAR. Not Tatar at all: a Paris steak à l'américaine of the early 1900s\, renamed. Best: Bistrot Paul Bert\, 11th — the old guide's pick\; not re-confirmed this autumn\, so call
LOCATION:Bistrot Paul Bert\, 11th — the old guide's pick\; not re-confirmed this autumn\, so call
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bistrot+Paul+Bert+18+rue+Paul+Bert+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-6@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Oeuf mayonnaise — A halved hard-boiled egg under a spoon of mayonnaise: the bistro's first plate.
DESCRIPTION:Say: uff ma-yo-NEZ. The cheapest line on the card and the truest test of a kitchen. Best: Bouillon Chartier\, faubourg Montmartre — since 1896
LOCATION:Bouillon Chartier\, faubourg Montmartre — since 1896
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Bouillon+Chartier+7+rue+du+Faubourg+Montmartre+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-7@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Choucroute garnie — Wine-braised sauerkraut under sausages\, bacon and steamed potatoes.
DESCRIPTION:Say: shoo-KROOT gar-NEE. Alsace's plate\, the house dish of the Left Bank's Alsatian brasserie since 1880. Best: Brasserie Lipp\, boulevard Saint-Germain
LOCATION:Brasserie Lipp\, boulevard Saint-Germain
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Brasserie+Lipp+151+boulevard+Saint-Germain+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-8@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Boeuf bourguignon — Beef braised for hours in red wine with lardons\, onions and mushrooms.
DESCRIPTION:Say: buff boor-geen-YON. The cheap\, generous main of the old crémeries and bouillons\, still cheap. Best: Polidor\, rue Monsieur-le-Prince — since 1845
LOCATION:Polidor\, rue Monsieur-le-Prince — since 1845
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Polidor+41+rue+Monsieur-le-Prince+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-9@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Riz au lait — Rice pudding brought to the table in a bowl the size of a basin\, with caramel and nougatine.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ree oh LAY. The dessert people book the Basque canteen by the Invalides for. Best: Chez L'Ami Jean\, rue Malar
LOCATION:Chez L'Ami Jean\, rue Malar
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Chez+L%27Ami+Jean+27+rue+Malar+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-10@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Galette complète — A buckwheat crêpe folded round ham\, cheese and an egg\, with a bowl of cider.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ga-LET kom-PLET. Brittany's crêpe\, made properly in the Marais and in Montparnasse\, where the Breton trains came in. Best: Breizh Café\, rue Vieille du Temple
LOCATION:Breizh Café\, rue Vieille du Temple
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Breizh+Cafe+109+rue+Vieille+du+Temple+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-11@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Falafel — Falafel in pita with fried aubergine\, cabbage\, tahini and hot sauce\, eaten on the pavement.
DESCRIPTION:Say: fa-la-FEL. The rue des Rosiers has fed the Marais from its windows for decades\; closed for the Sabbath. Best: L'As du Fallafel\, rue des Rosiers — closed Friday evening and Saturday
LOCATION:L'As du Fallafel\, rue des Rosiers — closed Friday evening and Saturday
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=L%27As+du+Fallafel+34+rue+des+Rosiers+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-12@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Croque-monsieur — Ham and cheese grilled between bread\, often under béchamel.
DESCRIPTION:Say: krok muh-SYUR. A café bite of about 1910 with a name that is a joke. Best: Café de Flore\, boulevard Saint-Germain — in rooms to linger
LOCATION:Café de Flore\, boulevard Saint-Germain — in rooms to linger
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cafe+de+Flore+172+boulevard+Saint-Germain+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-13@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Macaron — Two almond shells round a ganache\; the Ispahan is rose\, lychee and raspberry.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ma-ka-RON. The benchmark was set on the Left Bank\; eat one\, not a box. Best: Pierre Hermé\, rue Bonaparte — in the larder
LOCATION:Pierre Hermé\, rue Bonaparte — in the larder
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Pierre+Herme+72+rue+Bonaparte+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-14@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Paris-Brest — A ring of choux pastry filled with praline cream\, scattered with almonds.
DESCRIPTION:Say: pa-ree BREST. Made in 1910 for a bicycle race\, shaped like its wheel. Best: Pâtisserie Durand\, Maisons-Laffitte — the house of origin\; in the city\, the pastry houses in the larder
LOCATION:Pâtisserie Durand\, Maisons-Laffitte — the house of origin\; in the city\, the pastry houses in the larder
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Patisserie+Durand+avenue+de+Longueil+Maisons-Laffitte+France
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-15@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Opéra — Thin almond sponge soaked in coffee syrup\, coffee buttercream and chocolate ganache\, in flat layers.
DESCRIPTION:Say: o-pay-RA. Named for the Palais Garnier\, from the Dalloyau kitchen in 1955. Best: Dalloyau\, rue du Faubourg Saint-Honoré — the house of origin
LOCATION:Dalloyau\, rue du Faubourg Saint-Honoré — the house of origin
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Dalloyau+101+rue+du+Faubourg+Saint-Honore+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-16@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Grower Champagne — Champagne made and bottled by the family that grows the grapes: look for RM on the label.
DESCRIPTION:Say: sham-PAN-yuh. The nearest real vineyard to Paris\, an hour and a half east\, and the bottles the counters pour. Best: Le Verre Volé\, Canal Saint-Martin — in the bars
LOCATION:Le Verre Volé\, Canal Saint-Martin — in the bars
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Verre+Vole+67+rue+de+Lancry+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-17@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Beaujolais — Gamay from the granite hills\, drunk cool from a carafe.
DESCRIPTION:Say: bo-zho-LAY. The grape the natural-wine counters of Paris were built on. Best: Le Baratin\, Belleville
LOCATION:Le Baratin\, Belleville
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Le+Baratin+3+rue+Jouye-Rouve+Paris
END:VTODO
BEGIN:VTODO
UID:paris-liste-18@littoralicious.com
SUMMARY:Express au comptoir — A small black coffee taken standing at the zinc.
DESCRIPTION:Say: ex-PRESS oh kon-TWAR. Cheaper at the counter than at the table: the price is posted by law. Best: La Caféothèque\, by the Seine — in rooms to linger
LOCATION:La Caféothèque\, by the Seine — in rooms to linger
URL:https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Cafeotheque+52+rue+de+l%27Hotel+de+Ville+Paris
END:VTODO
END:VCALENDAR
